# Registry block media (images & video)

Use this when a block needs raster images (PNG, JPG, WebP) or video (MP4, WebM). Most blocks (navbars, SVG-only sections, etc.) do not need this.

## How it works

Media is served directly from `https://uiception.com`. There is no download step, no manifest file, and no `instrumentation.ts`. Consumers install a block and media works immediately with zero configuration.

## Consumer install

```bash
npx shadcn@latest add "https://uiception.com/r/<block-id>.json"
```

That's it. No extra setup required.

## Authoring: adding media to a block

### 1. Reference media in the entry file using `mediaOrigin`

Always declare `mediaOrigin` in the block's entry file (`<block-id>.tsx`) and build all media URLs through it. This makes the block work in **local dev** (before images are deployed) AND in **production / post-install**.

```tsx
// <block-id>.tsx — entry file

const mediaOrigin =
  process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_USE_LOCAL_BLOCK_MEDIA === "true"
    ? ""
    : "https://uiception.com"

const blockImage = (filename: string) =>
  `${mediaOrigin}/images/blocks/<block-id>/${filename}`

// video variant
const blockVideo = (filename: string) =>
  `${mediaOrigin}/videos/blocks/<block-id>/${filename}`
```

**`next/image`** — add `unoptimized` so consumers don't need `remotePatterns` in their `next.config`:

```tsx
import Image from "next/image"

<Image
  src={blockImage("bg.png")}
  unoptimized
  alt=""
  fill
/>
```

**Plain string constant** (shader props, video `src`, template helpers):

```tsx
const VIDEO_SRC = blockVideo("screen-demo.mp4")
```

### 2. Keep media URLs in the entry file — not in `config.ts`

`config.ts` holds pure data (types, labels, item arrays). Image/video URL construction belongs in the entry file alongside `mediaOrigin`.

```ts
// config.ts — pure data only
export const imageFiles = [
  { file: "photo-1.jpg", alt: "Description" },
  { file: "photo-2.jpg", alt: "Description" },
] as const
```

```tsx
// <block-id>.tsx — URL construction here
const items = imageFiles.map(({ file, alt }) => ({
  ...
  src: blockImage(file),
}))
```

### 3. No manifest, no registryDependency

- Do **not** create a `media-manifest.json` file.
- Do **not** add `https://uiception.com/r/uiception-media-fetch.json` to `registryDependencies`.
- Do **not** list binary files (`public/images/…`, `public/videos/…`) in `registry.json`.

### 4. Place assets in `public/` — Vercel serves them as the CDN

```
public/images/blocks/<block-id>/
  <block-id>-bg.png

public/videos/blocks/<block-id>/
  screen-demo.mp4
```

The `.env.local` in this repo already has `NEXT_PUBLIC_USE_LOCAL_BLOCK_MEDIA=true`, so local dev serves from `public/` automatically. Once deployed to Vercel, `https://uiception.com/images/blocks/…` resolves correctly for everyone.

## How local dev vs production resolves

| Environment | `NEXT_PUBLIC_USE_LOCAL_BLOCK_MEDIA` | `mediaOrigin` | Media served from |
|---|---|---|---|
| Local dev (`.env.local`) | `"true"` | `""` | `public/images/blocks/…` |
| Production / post-install | unset | `"https://uiception.com"` | `https://uiception.com/images/blocks/…` |

## What NOT to do

| ❌ Don't | ✅ Do |
|---|---|
| Hardcode `https://uiception.com/…` directly | Use `mediaOrigin` helper in the entry file |
| Put `mediaOrigin` or image URLs in `config.ts` | Keep `config.ts` as pure data; URLs in entry file |
| `media-manifest.json` in block folder | No manifest needed |
| `registryDependencies: ["…/uiception-media-fetch.json"]` | No dependency needed |
| `next/image` without `unoptimized` on external URL | Add `unoptimized` prop |
| List binary files in `registry.json` | Only list `.tsx`, `.ts`, `.css` source files |
